		<article class="max-w-4xl space-y-8 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour pt-10">
			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-3">
					Why Metro Manila Train Tracking Uses Both Crowdsourcing and Dispatch Math
				</h2>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed mb-3">
					Unlike transit authorities in Singapore, Tokyo, or London that publish open GTFS Realtime feeds every few seconds, Metro Manila rail operators currently run closed signaling systems without public API endpoints. Neither DOTr MRT-3, Light Rail Manila Corporation (LRMC) for LRT-1, nor LRTA for LRT-2 streams automated train telemetry directly to third-party commuter websites. When a transit app in the Philippines displays an estimated wait time on its own, that figure comes from deterministic schedule intervals rather than transponder hardware mounted inside the trainset.
				</p>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					Train Arrival PH bridges that data gap honestly. Whenever active passengers tap a check-in button on this page (such as reporting an arriving train, riding on board between stops, or flagging platform congestion), the directional cards surface that crowdsourced observation first under a clearly marked Rider Report indicator. When no recent rider reports exist within the trailing 15-minute window, the tracker never fabricates fake live pings: it openly displays Schedule estimate status backed by published terminal dispatches, station-to-station kilometer offsets, average commercial speeds, and typical dwell times.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-3">
					How Opt-In GPS Station Snapping Works Without Storing Your Location
				</h2>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed mb-3">
					Pressing the Use My GPS button lets commuters snap straight to their current platform without scrolling through 51 station names during rush hour. Your browser requests high-accuracy device coordinates strictly inside client-side memory and runs a Haversine distance check against the known coordinates of every MRT-3, LRT-1, and LRT-2 platform. If your phone is within 650 meters of a station entrance or viaduct, the tracker locks onto that station automatically and tags your next report as a GPS-verified station sighting.
				</p>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					Privacy remains enforced by design: your raw latitude and longitude never leave your browser and are never transmitted or written to any server or database column. Only the matched station identifier, rail line code, bound direction, report category, and an anonymous per-browser spam-control token are stored for a short 20-minute public display window before automatic cleanup removes older rows after two hours. If your device is farther than 650 meters from any rail station, GPS auto-snap declines to mark a live pin so remote users cannot accidentally skew platform wait estimates.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-3">
					Peak Rush Hours, Skip-Stop Mechanics, and Evening Gate Cutoffs
				</h2>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed mb-3">
					Headways across Metro Manila shift substantially between peak weekday commuter windows and midday or weekend operations. During morning rush hours (6:00 AM to 9:00 AM) and evening rush hours (5:00 PM to 8:00 PM), MRT-3 and LRT-1 typically insert extra trainsets to sustain 3.5 to 4.5 minute average headways. During off-peak hours (9:00 AM to 5:00 PM), headways widen to roughly 5 to 6.5 minutes on MRT-3 and LRT-1, and 6 to 7.5 minutes along LRT-2 between Recto and Antipolo.
				</p>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					Commuters catching late evening transfers should always check station passing windows carefully rather than relying only on origin terminal times. For example, although the extended weekday MRT-3 schedule dispatches its final northbound train from Taft Avenue at 11:09 PM, that train takes nearly 30 minutes to reach Cubao or Quezon Avenue, while southbound trains departing North Avenue leave as early as 10:30 PM. Concourse ticket gates close approximately 5 minutes prior to the final train arrival in each direction so station staff can sweep platforms safely.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-3">
					Planning Late-Night Transfers Across Cubao, Recto, and EDSA Taft
				</h2>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed mb-3">
					Because LRT-2 closes earlier than both MRT-3 and LRT-1 (final trains leave Antipolo at 9:00 PM daily, and Recto at 9:30 PM on weekdays or 9:00 PM on weekends), travelers heading home past 9:15 PM through Araneta Center-Cubao or Doroteo Jose often misjudge their transfer buffer. A passenger boarding MRT-3 at Ayala at 9:15 PM will arrive at Cubao around 9:35 PM, too late for the final westbound LRT-2 trip toward Recto and only barely catching or missing late eastbound trips passing through Cubao toward Marikina-Pasig and Antipolo.
				</p>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					Similarly, commuters transferring at EDSA Taft (southbound MRT-3 into LRT-1 toward PITX and Dr. Santos) need an extra 4 to 6 minutes to walk the elevated covered footbridge and queue at the LRT-1 stored-value tap gates. If the last LRT-1 trip has already passed your transfer station, the EDSA Carousel busway operating 24 hours a day directly beneath MRT-3 serves Monumento through PITX at regulated LTFRB fares.
				</p>
			</section>

